<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<CustomField xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
      THIS COMMENT IS INSIDE THE ROOT ELEMENT ON PURPOSE - a comment ABOVE the root breaks sf at
      source conversion with a misleading parent-xml error.

      RELAXED 2026-08-31 (GATE-B3): deleteConstraint Restrict to SetNull, and required true to
      false. Both halves are one change, not two - a REQUIRED lookup cannot carry SetNull, because
      the platform would have to null a field it also requires. Restrict is only reachable from
      required=true.

      WHY. With Restrict, a Transaction that has ANY Critical Date cannot be deleted, ever. Today
      only a demo seed script creates these rows, so the blast radius is small and it already bites:
      the delete in scripts/seed-transactions.apex throws on a rebuilt org for exactly this reason.
      Auto-creating critical dates on every deal would make DELETE_FAILED the default for every
      Transaction in the org and would remove the only cheap rollback a botched migration has -
      delete the deal and re-fan it.

      🔴 THE CONSEQUENCE THAT IS EASY TO MISS, AND IT IS AN FLS ONE. A required field is
      NON-PERMISSIONABLE: it cannot appear in a permission set fieldPermissions block, and that is
      why no DPEG permission set grants this field today. Making it optional turns it into an
      ordinary permissionable field, and a permissionable custom field with no FieldPermissions row
      is INVISIBLE - reads raise "No such column", which is an FLS denial wearing a schema error.
      DPEG_Checklist_Edit therefore grants it. Every OTHER permission set that grants
      Critical_Date__c object access needs the same grant in the same deploy; those are hub files a
      concurrent session is editing, so they are raised as a hub request rather than edited here.

      ⚠ WHAT WAS DELIBERATELY NOT DONE. No validation rule requiring the lookup ships with this
      change. Whether a SetNull cascade evaluates validation rules could not be measured this
      session (no org access), and a rule that DOES fire on the cascade would re-create the
      undeletable-parent defect with a worse error message than Restrict gave. Presence is enforced
      by the single service that creates these rows. Add the rule only after measuring the cascade.
    -->
    <fullName>Transaction__c</fullName>
    <deleteConstraint>SetNull</deleteConstraint>
    <description>The deal this critical date belongs to. Optional at the platform level with SetNull on purpose so a Transaction carrying critical dates stays deletable and a botched migration stays reversible; presence is enforced by the service that creates the rows, not by a required flag. See the XML comment in this file before changing either attribute - they move together.</description>
    <inlineHelpText>The deal this critical date belongs to. Normally set automatically when the date is projected from the deal.</inlineHelpText>
    <label>Transaction</label>
    <referenceTo>Transaction__c</referenceTo>
    <relationshipLabel>Critical Dates</relationshipLabel>
    <relationshipName>Critical_Dates</relationshipName>
    <required>false</required>
    <trackTrending>false</trackTrending>
    <type>Lookup</type>
</CustomField>
